title two ships, and what a restart must not lose
prompt print the updated petrichor command; pheromone is stopped and petrichor updates when its lap ends
think Checked both ships rather than trusting either report. Pheromone is genuinely stopped -- my own pgrep had been counting its own command line, which is the oldest false positive in the book. Petrichor runs at 5h12m with a parent and child pair on one tree, which is one claim rather than two.
think Petrichor stands clean: zero dirty, zero stashed, zero ahead, no rebase open. A reset costs it nothing.
think Pheromone holds unsent work it could not sign before today -- a session log and its index row from 113134, plus a stash the round-open took mid-rebase, whose On (no branch) line names exactly the state the %428 repair now clears. It signs now, proven by asking its own wrapper for a signature.
think That shapes the restart. A plain reset would take pheromone's modified index row, and sh reads a script as it executes, so resetting the tree under a running loop can corrupt the very file driving it. Both commands therefore update the tree BEFORE the launch reads fleet-loop.sh, and pheromone's stashes first.
obs Petrichor sits at 8cb601849a against xy at 924e861485, so it lacks the round-open repair. Its own round-open would adopt it, yet only after running the elder script once -- which is why the update belongs outside the loop rather than inside its first lap.
loom ships petrichor_uptime=5h12m petrichor_dirty=0 pheromone_dirty=2 pheromone_stash=1
file session-logs/date/20260905/20260905-155840\_two-ships-and-what-a-restart-must-not-lose.kyri this record
